ath kind of  reels with a lot of obstacles I went  through  I joined the nating went through boot  camp and then my a school and then from  there went to Budds which is the  precursor for seal training and I didn't  make it through my first time I went to  Iceland as a Navy policeman did that for  a while and worked really hard to be  able to get the opportunity to try to  become a seal again and second time I  went through I made it and I was pretty  much the path to to give me you know  obviously I get to you you get to your  SEAL team and you still have about six  months worth of training and advanced  training to do before you become a seal  but that initial six months of buzz  training is the toughest so in the  background of all this of course there's  your sexuality and you you of course had  to have been aware of what the policy  was in the military back in 1993 what  impact or how did that kind of interface  with your desires to be in the military  is that something you thought about yeah  I definitely thought about it you know  when I joined they had the the Don't Ask  Don't Tell policy was sort of new yeah  and I you know I knew instinctively at  the time that it was kind of a load of  crap so I just knew going in that I was  going to have to I'm going to have to do  a lot of line and is that what it was  was that you know some people talk about  their experience in the military under  Don't Ask Don't Tell as being more I  guess Don't Ask Don't Tell in the sense  that they didn't directly feel that they  had to lie they kind of fell out of  place when there may have been  conversations where they may not  necessarily have felt included based on  their sexuality but the direct line  wasn't really a part of it but for you  it was oh yeah yeah it definitely was  see the thing is is the SEAL team  community is a very tight-knit community  and there's not very many of us and so  you spend a lot of time and and at that  very small environment it gets very  difficult to keep you know a lie like  that or our secret like that from from  the guys you work with and what kinds of  situations would there be where you just  had to just lie  well I remember they kept a lot of the  guys kept asking me you know about you  know any girlfriends or anything like  that and I remember it got so bad that  at one point a friend of mine actually  posed as my girlfriend to a couple of  you know get-togethers that we had in  the Philippines so this wasn't in  pictures this was she actually would  show up places with you yeah yeah it was  it was like a comedy it was well  probably a comedy of errors more than  anything yeah well I mean I can  understand why there's this kind of  funny element of it looking back but at  the time what was that difficult for you  it was you know obviously just like  anybody you know you don't want to those  guys I mean we were so close to were  like brothers yeah and you know lying to  them just it was a it was a terrible  thing it made me feel really bad that I  would ever do that to them and and I'm  guessing it wouldn't have been  necessarily your choice right I mean you  did you feel that this is just the way  it had to be because of the policy it  definitely had to be that way the Don't  Ask Don't Tell policy it was a it was a  don't get caught policies all it really  was it was a it was a nightmare and he  is a mess were you were you out to your  family at the time that you joined the  Navy I was I was you and what was that  experience like in other words was that  is that something that you you did right  away or what was the timeline of that  like I came out uh I was sort of outed  my mom overheard a phone call that I had  with a friend of mine who was gay when I  was believed 17 I was fairly young and  it didn't go long my like I said my  father was a you know colonel in the Air  Force and you know they were very  religious so he felt very strongly  against it and yeah it just it wasn't a  very good coming out in fact I got  kicked out of my house  and then ultimately were you allowed  back in what was that what was that  period of time like uh it was tough it  was uh it was probably one of the most  toughest times in my life you know there  was a lot of heavy thinking after I got  kicked out of the house and you know I  did eventually move back in with my  parents just prior to me joining the  Navy but it was a it was tough it was I  learned a lot though you know there  wasn't all bad you know I think in any  situation where we're bad things sort of  happened to the person you kind of grow  and learn from it and become a little  bit stronger now since your dad was in  the Air Force he of course must have  also been aware of what the policy was  so were there conversations with him  about your potential risk situation  there I don't you know honestly don't  recall a specific conversation I think  it was something the morale that we just  didn't talk about in fact after you know  after that you know getting kicked out  of the house we didn't we didn't talk  about my sexuality for years and when  you uh before leaving the Navy SEALs had  you come out yeah I was I was forced out  of the closet see I was a 2001 or no I'm  sorry yet 2002 rather I just got back  from doing a six-month deployment we  deployed two weeks after 9/11 and I got  back and I was the person I was living  with at the time when I was in a  relationship with had this big party for  me for you know coming back and you know  being healthy and you know all that and  I drank a little bit at that party and  then the next day you know I woke up and  I had realized that before I went to bed  that I probably didn't spend as much  time with him as I should have instead  you know talking with other you know  people at the party and I left I called  his office and left a message on the  machine and  is one of the people that work for him  heard that message and it was somebody  that he had recently just counse